# The kernel needs to be updated to get wireguard to work. Use the instructions below.
sudo dnf install wireguard-tools
sudo dnf install elrepo-release epel-release
sudo dnf --enablerepo=elrepo-kernel install kernel-ml
uname -r # to see if the kernel has been upgraded
sudo reboot
uname -r # to see if the kernel has been upgraded
#sudo dnf install kmod-wireguard
sudo modprobe wireguard
lsmod | grep wireguard # confirm it is installed
sudo dnf clean all
sudo dnf update
sudo dnf install --allowerasing kernel-devel kernel-headers

OK, we still can't ping a VM on in16 from in07 but we're getting closer.
We can see the ping show up in the tcpdump on in16 in both the wg0 interface and the virbr1 interface.
But it's not going anywhere from the virbr1 interface it seems like. We were able to achieve the above
by setting the following:
sudo iptables -I LIBVIRT_FWI 1 -i wg0 -o virbr1 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -I LIBVIRT_FWO 1 -i virbr1 -o wg0 -j ACCEPT
Note that virbr1 is network interface to which the libvirt hostonly-net is attached.

On in07, we have to add a route to the VMs network:
sudo ip route add 192.168.56.0/24 dev wg0

The above three things have been added to the ansible playbook. Then just one more thing needs to be 
done which is go into the VM and do this:
sudo ip route add 192.68.2.0/24 via 192.168.56.1

Then once that is done, we can ping that VM from in07.
